; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; m2snes engine image.
;
; This is our own original 65816 code. It contains no data from anyone's ROM,
; and it is assembled at DEV TIME into `engine.bin` + `engine.sym`, both of
; which are committed. An end user running `m2snes their-rom.gb` never needs an
; assembler: the shipped Zig binary embeds this image, drops the converted
; assets into the regions `src/snes_layout.zig` reserves, and patches the tables
; at `RegionTable` and `BootRecord` below so the code can find them.
;
; The patch tables are the whole interface between the two halves. The engine
; never hardcodes a region address, because the assembler and the layout
; manifest are separate sources of truth and a hardcoded address is a drift
; waiting to happen. Each table carries a magic word and a version so the
; injector can refuse to patch an image that does not match the manifest it was
; built against, rather than writing pointers into the wrong offsets.
;
; Phase 0a scope, as of Step 12: boot, set up Mode 1 with the play field on BG3,
; mask a 160x144 window out of the 256x224 frame, replay one converted door
; script into VRAM, draw one converted screen, and move a camera over it under
; the original's clamps and scroll-block flags. There is no Samus and nothing
; moves on its own; that is Step 13.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

lorom
arch 65816

; ---- Hardware registers ---------------------------------------------------
!INIDISP  = $2100
!OBSEL    = $2101
!BGMODE   = $2105
!MOSAIC   = $2106
!BG1SC    = $2107
!BG2SC    = $2108
!BG3SC    = $2109
!BG4SC    = $210A
!BG12NBA  = $210B
!BG34NBA  = $210C
!BG1HOFS  = $210D
!BG1VOFS  = $210E
!BG2HOFS  = $210F
!BG2VOFS  = $2110
!BG3HOFS  = $2111
!BG3VOFS  = $2112
!BG4HOFS  = $2113
!BG4VOFS  = $2114
!VMAIN    = $2115
!VMADDL   = $2116
!VMADDH   = $2117
!VMDATAL  = $2118
!VMDATAH  = $2119
!RDVRAML  = $2139
!RDVRAMH  = $213A
!OAMADDL  = $2102
!OAMADDH  = $2103
!OAMDATA  = $2104
!CGADD    = $2121
!CGDATA   = $2122
!W12SEL   = $2123
!W34SEL   = $2124
!WOBJSEL  = $2125
!WH0      = $2126
!WH1      = $2127
!WH2      = $2128
!WH3      = $2129
!WBGLOG   = $212A
!WOBJLOG  = $212B
!TM       = $212C
!TS       = $212D
!TMW      = $212E
!TSW      = $212F
!CGWSEL   = $2130
!CGADSUB  = $2131
!COLDATA  = $2132
!SETINI   = $2133
!WMDATA   = $2180
!WMADDL   = $2181
!WMADDM   = $2182
!WMADDH   = $2183
!NMITIMEN = $4200
!MDMAEN   = $420B
!HDMAEN   = $420C
!MEMSEL   = $420D
!VTIMEL   = $4209
!VTIMEH   = $420A
!RDNMI    = $4210
!TIMEUP   = $4211
!HVBJOY   = $4212
!JOY1     = $4218

; DMA channel 0, and channel 7 for the window HDMA.
!DMAP0    = $4300
!BBAD0    = $4301
!A1T0L    = $4302
!A1B0     = $4304
!DAS0L    = $4305
!DMAP3    = $4330
!BBAD3    = $4331
!A1T3L    = $4332
!A1B3     = $4334
!DMAP4    = $4340
!BBAD4    = $4341
!A1T4L    = $4342
!A1B4     = $4344
!DMAP5    = $4350
!BBAD5    = $4351
!A1T5L    = $4352
!A1B5     = $4354
!DMAP6    = $4360
!BBAD6    = $4361
!A1T6L    = $4362
!A1B6     = $4364
!DMAP7    = $4370
!BBAD7    = $4371
!A1T7L    = $4372
!A1B7     = $4374

; ---- Video decisions, mirrored from src/snes_target.zig -------------------
; These are the only numbers duplicated between the assembler and Zig, and each
; is checked against `snes_target` and `snes_screen` by tests in
; `src/snes_inject.zig` reading this image back out, so a change on either side
; is caught rather than discovered on hardware.
!VIEW_W       = 160
!VIEW_H       = 144
!SCREEN_W     = 256
!SCREEN_H     = 224
!WIN_LEFT     = (!SCREEN_W-!VIEW_W)/2            ; 48
!WIN_RIGHT    = !WIN_LEFT+!VIEW_W-1              ; 207
!BAND_TOP     = (!SCREEN_H-!VIEW_H)/2            ; 40
!BAND_BOTTOM  = ((!SCREEN_H-!VIEW_H)-((!SCREEN_H-!VIEW_H)/2))  ; 40

; BGMODE 1. BG3 priority bit left clear: the play field is the world, not an
; overlay above it.
!MODE          = $01
; Every play-field word carries BG3's tile priority bit, Step 24e. In mode 1
; that is the only way an object can go under BG3: OBJ priority 0 sits between
; BG3's two levels. The Game Boy's behind-background bit becomes priority 0 and
; its absence priority 2 (`PutObject`), so this bit is what makes the first one
; mean something. `snes_target.play_priority` bakes it into converted words;
; the engine's own writers OR it in.
!PLAY_PRI      = $2000
; BG3SC: tilemap at VRAM word $0800, 32x32. BG2SC: word $0C00.
!BG3_MAP       = $0800
!BG3SC_VALUE   = (!BG3_MAP>>10)<<2
!BG2_MAP       = $0C00
!BG2SC_VALUE   = (!BG2_MAP>>10)<<2
!BG12_CHAR_BASE = $1000
; BG34NBA: BG3 characters at word $0000.
!BG34NBA_VALUE = $00
; OBSEL: object characters at word $6000 ($2000-word steps), 8x8/16x16 sprites.
; The Game Boy's objects here are 8x8 - LCDC is written $E3, and bit 2 clear is
; OBJ8 - so "small" is what every sprite the engine composes selects, and the
; large size is never used.
!OBJ_CHAR_BASE = $6000
!OBSEL_VALUE   = !OBJ_CHAR_BASE>>13
; BG12NBA: BG1 at word $1000, and **BG2 on the objects' characters** (Step 24g).
; The Game Boy's window reads $8800-$8FFF through LCDC bit 4's signed mode,
; which are the bytes its objects read as $80-$FF: one copy serves both. BG2 is
; 4bpp, the objects' depth, so pointing it at them gives the window the status
; bar's tiles ($9C-$AF, Samus's sheet), $FF (the common items' last), and the
; item font at $C0 exactly when `ITEM` or a load has put it there -- which is
; when the Game Boy's window has it too. BG1, the readout, keeps its own.
!BG2_CHAR_BASE = !OBJ_CHAR_BASE
!BG12NBA_VALUE = ((!BG2_CHAR_BASE>>12)<<4)|(!BG12_CHAR_BASE>>12)
; Main screen: BG2 (HUD), BG3 (play field), OBJ. BG1 is reserved for the wider
; view and stays off in Phase 0a.
!TM_VALUE      = $16

; ---- The camera, mirrored from src/snes_screen.zig ------------------------
; The camera coordinate is the viewport centre, and !CamX / !CamY each hold a
; whole world position: the screen nibble in the high byte, the pixel in the
; low one. That is the original's `(screen, pixel)` pair, and packing it into
; one word is what makes a screen crossing free - a 16-bit add carries out of
; the pixel byte into the screen byte by itself, so nothing has to notice.
;
; These clamps are NOT the limits of that travel. They apply only on an edge the
; cell's scroll byte blocks; an open edge has no limit at all. In the original a
; blocked edge is where a door transition starts, which is Step 15.
!SCREEN_SPAN  = 256
!HUD_H        = 8
!CAM_MIN_X    = !VIEW_W/2                        ; 80
!CAM_MAX_X    = !SCREEN_SPAN-(!VIEW_W/2)         ; 176
!CAM_MIN_Y    = !VIEW_H/2                        ; 72
; The downward clamp carries the original's `+ $08`: the bottom eight lines of
; the Game Boy's window are the HUD band, so the camera is allowed that much
; further down. `handleCamera` writes it as `$100 - SCRN_Y/2 + $08`.
!CAM_MAX_Y    = (!SCREEN_SPAN-(!VIEW_H/2))+(!HUD_H)  ; 192
!CAM_STEP     = 2
; Where `prepMapUpdate` fetches the row or column it is about to write, relative
; to the camera. Three of the four reach $30 past it and the rightward one $20;
; the asymmetry is the original's.
!STREAM_BACK_X  = (!CAM_MIN_X)+$30               ; 128
!STREAM_BACK_Y  = (!CAM_MIN_Y)+$30               ; 120
!STREAM_AHEAD_X = (!CAM_MIN_X)+$20               ; 112
; Which directions owe a row or column. One is serviced per frame, round-robin
; on the low two bits of the frame counter, so a 16-pixel metatile has four
; frames to arrive - which is what bounds !CAM_STEP.
!STREAM_UP    = $01
!STREAM_DOWN  = $02
!STREAM_LEFT  = $04
!STREAM_RIGHT = $08
; Phase 0a has no Samus to follow, so the camera starts in the middle of the
; screen - which is inside the clamps at every play-window size that leaves any
; travel at all.
!CAM_START_X  = !SCREEN_SPAN/2
!CAM_START_Y  = !SCREEN_SPAN/2
; BGnHOFS names the world pixel drawn at screen column zero, so the camera has
; to be biased back by half a window plus where the window starts. The vertical
; bias carries the PPU's extra line: it fetches the row *after* the one VOFS
; names.
!SCROLL_X_BIAS = (!CAM_MIN_X)+(!WIN_LEFT)            ; 128
!SCROLL_Y_BIAS = (!CAM_MIN_Y)+(!BAND_TOP)+1          ; 113

; Joypad bits in the 16-bit auto-read at $4218.
!PAD_UP       = $0800
!PAD_DOWN     = $0400
!PAD_LEFT     = $0200
!PAD_RIGHT    = $0100
!PAD_A        = $0080
!PAD_B        = $8000
!PAD_X        = $0040
!PAD_START    = $1000
; The item toggle. Select on both machines, which is the one Game Boy button
; whose SNES counterpart needs no argument about where it should go.
!PAD_SELECT   = $2000

; `BootMode`. Defined up here rather than beside `!BOOT_VERSION` with the record
; because asar resolves a define at the point it is used, and `TitleScreen` --
; the one routine that reads the mode -- is five thousand lines above it.
!BOOT_NEW_GAME = 1
!PAD_Y        = $4000
; The Game Boy's two buttons, mapped by role rather than by position: jump is
; the lower face button and fire the left one, which is where Super Metroid puts
; them and therefore where a player coming from the rando expects them. Named
; here so a remap is one line rather than a search.
!PAD_JUMP     = !PAD_B
!PAD_FIRE     = !PAD_Y
; The shoulders, which no Game Boy button maps to and no rung hands the cart.
; Held together they toggle the room readout (Step 14).
!PAD_L        = $0020
!PAD_R        = $0010

; ---- Samus, mirrored from M2RoS SRC/constants.asm and src/physics.zig -----
; Poses. Phase 0a implements the ones marked; the rest are named so the
; dispatch can say which one it was handed.
!POSE_STAND      = $00
!POSE_JUMP       = $01          ; implemented
!POSE_SPINJUMP   = $02          ; implemented
!POSE_RUN        = $03          ; implemented
!POSE_CROUCH     = $04          ; implemented
!POSE_MORPH      = $05          ; implemented
!POSE_BALLJUMP   = $06          ; implemented: the ball on a jump arc
!POSE_BALLFALL   = $08
!POSE_FALL       = $07          ; implemented
!POSE_NJUMPSTART = $09          ; implemented
!POSE_SPINSTART  = $0A          ; implemented
!POSE_FACESCREEN = $13          ; implemented: the appearance sequence a new game opens on
; The spider ball's four (Step 14b). **These were named `SPRINGDROP`,
; `SPRINGJUMP` and `SPRINGFALL` until 2026-09-15, and the names were wrong.** The
; 2026-09-02 note here read the three stores right and the test in front of them
; wrong: every Down arm that makes one is `LD A,($D045) / BIT 5,A`, and bit 5 is
; Spider Ball's (00:$1254, $1789, $17A9, $0ED4). While `!ITEM_SPRING` was $20 the
; arms tested the right bit under the wrong name; Step 11's mask fix made the name
; right and the arms wrong, and nothing could show it until Spider Ball was held.
; `pose_spider` is `poseFunc_spiderBall` (00:$1029), the ball at rest.
!POSE_SPIDERROLL = $0B          ; implemented, 00:$1083
!POSE_SPIDERFALL = $0C          ; implemented, 00:$11E4
!POSE_SPIDERJUMP = $0D          ; implemented, 00:$1170
!POSE_SPIDER     = $0E          ; implemented, 00:$1029
; `collision_checkSpiderSet`'s probe offsets from her position bytes, each an
; `ADD A,d8` in 00:$1A42 and checked there by `src/correspond.zig`; the middles
; are the ROM's own operands, not averages taken here.
!SPIDER_X_LEFT   = $0A          ; 00:$1A73
!SPIDER_X_RIGHT  = $15          ; 00:$1A48
!SPIDER_X_MID    = $0F          ; 00:$1AD9
!SPIDER_Y_TOP    = $1E          ; 00:$1A4F
!SPIDER_Y_BOTTOM = $2C          ; 00:$1A61
!SPIDER_Y_MID    = $25          ; 00:$1AAA
; `spiderDirectionTable`'s four rows: counter-clockwise first and second tries,
; then clockwise. The row is the contact nibble.
!SPIDER_TRY1     = $00          ; 00:$10B7 `LD HL,$20A9`
!SPIDER_TRY2     = $10          ; 00:$10FD `LD HL,$20B9`
!SPIDER_CW       = $20          ; 00:$10BF `LD HL,$20C9`, from either try
; B4b's four. $0F and $10 are what `samus_damagePoseTable` puts her in; both
; fall through into $11, which is where the knockback arc is actually flown, and
; $12 is $11's ball, with two arms of its own in front (00:$0ECB). $18 is named for the two tests that compare against it and
; is not implemented: her room is entered since 1.0 Step 6, and being eaten is
; Step 20's.
!POSE_HURT       = $0F          ; implemented
!POSE_MORPHHURT  = $10          ; implemented
!POSE_BOMBED     = $11          ; implemented
!POSE_MORPHBOMBED = $12         ; implemented, 00:$0ECB into $11's body
!POSE_EATEN      = $18          ; 1.0 Step 20a: `poseFunc_beingEaten`, 00:$0E36
!POSE_INMOUTH    = $19          ; 1.0 Step 20a: 00:$0DF0
!POSE_TOSTOMACH  = $1A          ; 1.0 Step 20a: 00:$0DBE
!POSE_INSTOMACH  = $1B          ; 1.0 Step 20a: 00:$0D87
!POSE_OUTSTOMACH = $1C          ; 1.0 Step 20a: 00:$0D8B
!POSE_EXITQUEEN  = $1D          ; 1.0 Step 20a: `poseFunc_morphBombed`'s, 00:$0ECB
; The turnaround is the running pose with bit 7 set, not a pose of its own.
!POSE_TURN       = $80|!POSE_RUN

; Where a screen-relative position becomes an OAM one. The collision arithmetic
; runs through both and `getTilemapAddress` takes them straight back off, so
; they appear on the way in and on the way out and cancel.
!OAM_Y_OFS = 16
!OAM_X_OFS = 8

; Samus's hitbox, from `constants.asm`, which derives all of it from the two
; `toCenter` values.
!ORIGIN_X_CENTER     = $08
!ORIGIN_Y_CENTER     = $0A
!ORIGIN_X_LEFT       = !ORIGIN_X_CENTER-$05
!ORIGIN_X_RIGHT      = !ORIGIN_X_CENTER+$04
!ORIGIN_Y_STAND      = !ORIGIN_Y_CENTER-$12
!ORIGIN_Y_STAND_CHECK = !ORIGIN_Y_STAND+$08
!ORIGIN_Y_BOTTOM     = !ORIGIN_Y_CENTER+$12
; The row `samus_tryUnmorph` probes: the crouch's head, eight pixels below
; the standing check, which is why unmorphing fits where standing does not.
!ORIGIN_Y_BALL_TOP   = !ORIGIN_Y_CENTER-$02

; Where the jump arc starts being read. Below this the ascent is linear and the
; table is not consulted at all.
!JUMP_BASE = $40

; Block-type bits in a collision table entry. **All eight, since Step 12a**;
; Phase 0a read three and stopped at bit 4, because the rest need
; damage, items or projectiles to mean anything.
;
; The five that were missing are named from the ROM's own tests rather than
; from a constants file, which is arm two's step 0a -- a transcribed constant
; is a number someone typed until something in the cartridge is made to say it.
; Every bit below is a `BIT n,A` on the byte a `LD H,$DC / LD L,id / LD A,(HL)`
; has just read, and there are fourteen such sites in the ROM:
;
;   bit 0  00:$1EB5  water:  `LD A,$FF / LD ($D048),A`
;   bit 1  00:$1EBF  up:     `CCF` -- it *inverts* the solidity it was handed
;   bit 2  00:$1F59  down:   the same inversion on the other probe
;   bit 3  00:$2021  spike:  `hurtFlag` 1, boost 0, damage from `$D078`
;   bit 4  00:$1EC5  acid:   `$40` into `$D062`, and acid damage from `$D077`
;   bit 5  01:$5176  shot:   `LD A,$FF / CALL destroyBlock`
;   bit 6  01:$553F  bomb:   the same, from five bomb arms rather than one beam
;   bit 7  00:$1F4F  save:   `LD A,$FF / LD ($D07D),A`
;
; `ConstBlockShot` and `ConstBlockBomb` are exported below and checked against
; those two opcodes by `src/correspond.zig`; the other six have no reader that
; could tell a wrong bit from a right one yet, and are written down here.
!BLOCK_WATER = $01
!BLOCK_UP    = $02              ; half-solid floor: passable going up
!BLOCK_DOWN  = $04              ; half-solid ceiling: passable going down
!BLOCK_SPIKE = $08              ; 00:$2021, and the damage is the door's `$6x`
!BLOCK_ACID  = $10              ; acid: damage every 16th frame, from `$D077`.
                                ; Named `!BLOCK_ACID` until Step 22, from a
                                ; misreading, and the damage was never ported
!BLOCK_SHOT  = $20              ; destroyed by the beam. Step 12a's
!BLOCK_BOMB  = $40              ; destroyed by a bomb, and by nothing else
!BLOCK_SAVE  = $80              ; a save station's tile

; Item bits, from `constants.asm`. Phase 0a grants none of them.
; **Corrected in Step 11; two of these were the wrong bit for two phases.**
; `!ITEM_BOMB` read $10 and `!ITEM_SPRING` read $20, which are Spring Ball's
; bit and Spider Ball's - and Spider Ball had no mask at all. Nine branches in
; the pose machine read them and none could show it, because `!Items` is zero
; for the whole of Phase 0a. The authority is the cartridge rather than a
; constants file: `handleItemPickup`'s seven equipment arms are
; `ld a,[samusItems] / set n,a / ld [samusItems],a`, and `src/items.zig` reads
; `n` out of the opcode. See `docs/bug_tracker.md`, 2026-09-09.
!ITEM_BOMB   = $01
!ITEM_HIJUMP = $02
!ITEM_SCREW  = $04
!ITEM_SPACE  = $08
!ITEM_SPRING = $10
!ITEM_SPIDER = $20
!ITEM_VARIA  = $40

; The y-offset rows the horizontal collision walks: eight bytes of stride, and
; a reader unrolled five deep, so five is what a row can carry.
!YOFF_STRIDE = 8
!YOFF_MAX    = 5

; The camera's guides: where in camera space the camera wants Samus to be, and
; the bias that puts her there. From `handleCamera`, which spells them as
; `SCRN_X/2 + OAM_X_OFS + samusOriginX_toCenter` and so on.
!GUIDE_BIAS_X       = (!VIEW_W/2)+!OAM_X_OFS+!ORIGIN_X_CENTER      ; $60
!GUIDE_BIAS_Y       = (!VIEW_H/2)+!OAM_Y_OFS+!ORIGIN_Y_CENTER-2    ; $60
!GUIDE_RIGHT        = !OAM_X_OFS+$38                               ; $40
!GUIDE_LEFT         = !VIEW_W+!OAM_X_OFS-$38                       ; $70
!GUIDE_DOWN_OPEN    = $50
!GUIDE_DOWN_BLOCKED = $40
!GUIDE_UP_OPEN      = $4E
!GUIDE_UP_BLOCKED   = $3E

; ---- Class indexes, mirrored from src/snes_layout.zig's Class enum --------
!CLASS_CHR_BG      = 0

; `snes_convert.CopyClass`, the low nibble of a converted COPY. Only the two the
; frame cost turns on are named; `CopyClassToRegion` is what the rest are for.
!COPY_BG      = 1
!COPY_OBJ     = 2
!COPY_BG_TWIN = 3
!CLASS_CHR_OBJ     = 1
!CLASS_TILEMAP     = 2
!CLASS_METATILES   = 3
!CLASS_COLLISION   = 4
!CLASS_SOLIDITY    = 5
!CLASS_MAP_CELLS   = 6
!CLASS_MAP_SCREENS = 7
!CLASS_DOORS       = 8
!CLASS_DOOR_PTRS   = 9
!CLASS_DIRECTORY   = 10
!CLASS_PHYSICS     = 11
!CLASS_METASPRITES = 12
!CLASS_ENEMIES     = 13
!CLASS_ARAM        = 14
!CLASS_TITLE_ART   = 15
!CLASS_DEBUG       = 16

; Blob ids within !CLASS_DEBUG, mirrored from `src/debug_tables.zig` (1.0 Step
; 4): the METROIDS and FLAGS lists. A count byte, then per entry the map bank,
; the cell, the spawn number and a zero-padded label.
!DEBUG_BLOB_METROIDS = 0
!DEBUG_QUEEN_BANK = $00         ; 1.0 Step 10: `debug_tables.queen_bank`, the Queen's row
!DEBUG_BLOB_FLAGS    = 1
!DEBUG_BLOB_WARP_SAVES = 2      ; 1.0 Step 5b: the WARP page's four lists,
!DEBUG_BLOB_WARP_ITEMS = 3      ;   `debug_tables.warp_lists`
!DEBUG_BLOB_WARP_METROIDS = 4
!DEBUG_BLOB_WARP_QUEEN = 5
!DEBUG_BLOB_WARP_DATA = 6       ; and what their rows run
!DEBUG_BLOB_LARVAE = 7          ; 1.0 Step 10: `debug_tables.larvaeBlob`
!DEBUG_LARVAE_ROWS = 4          ;   and where its per-row bytes start
!WARP_BYTES          = 16       ; `debug_tables.warp_bytes`
!WARP_SWEEP          = $C0      ; the loader's window, bottom seam to top: $68 + $58
!WARP_STEP           = $08      ; half the loader's $10 grid row: see `DebugWarp`
!DEBUG_ENTRY         = 24       ; `debug_tables.entry_bytes`
!DEBUG_LABEL         = 3        ; `debug_tables.header_bytes`

; Blob ids within !CLASS_TITLE_ART, mirrored from `src/title_super.zig` (Step
; 24j): the characters, the palette, and the map patch, whose header is the
; patch's word offset in BG1's map, its columns and rows, and the first
; character id.
!TITLE_ART_CHR    = 0
!TITLE_ART_PAL    = 1
!TITLE_ART_MAP    = 2
!TITLE_ART_HEADER = 6           ; `title_super.map_header_bytes`
!TITLE_ART_CGRAM  = 7*16        ; BG palette 7, `title_super.palette`

; Blob ids within !CLASS_PHYSICS, mirrored from src/physics.zig's `Which`.
!PHYS_FALL       = 0
!PHYS_JUMP       = 1
!PHYS_SPACE_JUMP = 2
!PHYS_HITBOX_TOP = 3
!PHYS_Y_OFFSETS  = 4
!PHYS_BOMB       = 5
!PHYS_SPR_HITBOX = 6
!PHYS_DMG_POSE   = 7
!PHYS_BOMBED_POSE = 8
; And B5's eleven, Step 12b. Same class, and the class is what the engine looks
; a small table up in rather than a claim about what the table means.
!PHYS_SHOT_DIRS  = 9
!PHYS_SHOT_PRI   = 10
!PHYS_CANNON_X   = 11
!PHYS_CANNON_Y_P = 12
!PHYS_CANNON_Y_A = 13
!PHYS_WAVE_SPD   = 14
!PHYS_MISS_SPD   = 15
!PHYS_MISS_TILE  = 16
!PHYS_MISS_ATTR  = 17
!PHYS_BEAM_SND   = 18
!PHYS_WPN_DMG    = 19
; Step 12f's.
!PHYS_HOP_ARC_Y  = 20
!PHYS_HOP_ARC_X  = 21
!PHYS_GUL_Y      = 22
!PHYS_GUL_X      = 23
!PHYS_LEECH_X    = 24
!PHYS_LEECH_Y    = 25
!PHYS_ACCEL_FWD  = 26
!PHYS_ACCEL_BACK = 27
; Step 12c's: the pose an explosion throws Samus into, per pose she was in.
!PHYS_BOMB_POSE  = 28
; Step 13b's: `hudBaseTilemap`, the status bar's twenty fixed cells.
!PHYS_HUD_BASE   = 29
; Step 13c's: the Alpha's angle table and its sixteen speed vectors.
!PHYS_ALPHA_ANGLES = 30
!PHYS_ALPHA_SPEEDS = 31
!PHYS_SPIDER_DIRS = 32
!PHYS_SPIDER_ORIENT = 33
; Step 15a's: the two pointer tables a save record's pointers come from.
!PHYS_META_PTRS = 34
!PHYS_COLL_PTRS = 35
; Step 15c's: the death's erase offsets and the GAME OVER text.
!PHYS_DEATH_ERASE = 36
!PHYS_GAME_OVER_TEXT = 37
; Step 24g's: the window's second row at boot, and `ITEM`'s names for it.
!PHYS_SAVE_TEXT = 38
!PHYS_ITEM_NAMES = 39
; 1.0 Step 2a's: the pause's L counter.
!PHYS_L_COUNTER = 40
; 1.0 Step 6's: the Queen's three head frames.
!PHYS_QUEEN_HEAD = 41
; 1.0 Step 11's: the skreek's jump and the drivel's swoop.
!PHYS_SKREEK_JUMP = 42
!PHYS_DRIVEL_Y = 43
!PHYS_DRIVEL_X = 44
!PHYS_SINE_CONCAVE = 45
!PHYS_SINE_CONVEX = 46
; 1.0 Step 12's: the blob thrower's parts and tables, and its blobs' moves.
!PHYS_BLOB_THROWER = 47
!PHYS_BLOB_MOVES = 48
; 1.0 Step 13's: Arachnus's three jump tables, one run.
!PHYS_ARACH_JUMP = 49
; 1.0 Step 14's: the Gamma's angle table and its twenty-four speed arms.
!PHYS_GAMMA_ANGLES = 50
!PHYS_GAMMA_SPEEDS = 51
; 1.0 Step 15's: `enemy_seekSamus`'s speed table (03:$6BB1), the Zeta's and
; the Omega's and the larvae's.
!PHYS_SEEK_SPEEDS = 52
; 1.0 Step 19b's: the Queen's neck patterns, her feet, her state list and her
; walk.
!PHYS_QUEEN_NECK = 53
!PHYS_QUEEN_FEET = 54
!PHYS_QUEEN_STATES = 55
!PHYS_QUEEN_WALK = 56
; 1.0 Step 20b's: her bent neck's five objects.
!PHYS_QUEEN_BENT = 57
; 1.0 Step 22: the credits' fade, their stars and their text.
!PHYS_CREDITS_FADE = 58
!PHYS_CREDITS_STARS = 59
!PHYS_CREDITS_TEXT = 60

; Blob ids within !CLASS_METASPRITES, mirrored from src/sprites.zig's `Which`.
!MS_PTRS         = 0
!MS_DATA         = 1
!MS_POSE_JUMP    = 2
!MS_POSE_SPIN    = 3
!MS_POSE_STAND   = 4
!MS_POSE_RUN     = 5
; The enemy set. Extracted and round-tripped since Step 4 and **not shipped into
; the cart until Step 12d**, which is the whole of why the enemies were
; invisible: their slots were filled, walked, collided against and damaged, and
; no blob in the cart held a part list for any of them.
!MS_EN_PTRS      = 6
!MS_EN_DATA      = 7
; The credits set, Step 24h: `drawNonGameSprite`'s, which the title's menu is
; drawn from.
!MS_CR_PTRS      = 8
!MS_CR_DATA      = 9

; Blob ids within !CLASS_ENEMIES, mirrored from src/entity.zig's `Which`.
!EN_DATA_ID      = 0
!EN_PTRS_ID      = 1
!EN_HDRS_ID      = 2
!EN_HPTRS_ID     = 3
!EN_DMG_ID       = 4
!EN_HBOX_ID      = 5
!EN_XPTRS_ID     = 6

; The two poses that have no sprite-id table: `drawSamus_jumpStart` and
; `drawSamus_crouch` load an immediate. From M2RoS's generated
; sprites_samusConstants.asm, and pinned by the same next-routine arithmetic as
; the tables - the crouch's $0B is literally the `3E 0B` at 01:4D65 that
; identifies the routine.
!SPR_FACE_SCREEN = $00
!SPR_JUMPSTART_R = $03          ; SPRITE_SAMUS_RUN_RIGHT_1
!SPR_JUMPSTART_L = $10          ; SPRITE_SAMUS_RUN_LEFT_1
!SPR_CROUCH_R    = $0B
!SPR_CROUCH_L    = $18
; The ball, 01:$4C94, which is a table of eight rather than a pair of
; immediates: four rolling frames facing left then four facing right. Both runs
; are consecutive, so two bases and an index reproduce it exactly and there is
; no table of ROM bytes to keep. The index is bits 3-2 of the same counter
; `HandlePose` advances at the top of every frame, so the ball rolls one frame
; every four.
!SPR_BALL_L      = $1E
; The knockback, 01:$4C59, which indexes a two-byte table at 01:$4C69 by facing.
; Two bytes is a pair of immediates by any other name, so they are constants
; here for the same reason the crouch's are - and the pair is `16 09` at that
; address in the cartridge, which is `SPRITE_SAMUS_JUMP_LEFT` then
; `SPRITE_SAMUS_JUMP_RIGHT`. Poses $0F and $11 draw with them; $10 and $12 draw
; as the ball.
!SPR_KNOCK_L     = $16
!SPR_KNOCK_R     = $09
!SPR_BALL_R      = $26
; The spider ball, 01:$4C8C: the ball's shape exactly -- eight ids, two runs of
; four, the same counter -- and a different eight. `pose_sprites_spider`.
!SPR_SPIDER_L    = $37
!SPR_SPIDER_R    = $3B

; Where the three running tables sit inside the one blob: normal, firing
; forwards, aiming up, eight bytes each.
!RUN_NORMAL      = $00
!RUN_SHOOTING    = $08
!RUN_AIMING_UP   = $10
; And the two spin tables inside theirs.
!SPIN_RIGHT      = $00
!SPIN_LEFT       = $04

; A converted map cell is four bytes: screen index, scroll flags, transition.
!CELL_BYTES     = 4
!SCREEN_BYTES   = 256
!NULL_SCREEN    = $FF

; ---- Low RAM --------------------------------------------------------------
; Direct page $00xx holds the engine's variables; $0800-$0FFF is the tilemap
; staging buffer, which is exactly one 32x32 map; $1000-$121F is the OAM shadow;
; the stack lives above that and below $2000, where the WRAM mirror ends.
!FrameCount = $00       ; 2
!MapIndex   = $02
!Cell       = $03
!Scroll     = $04
!TileTable  = $05
!Redraw     = $06
!CamX       = $08       ; 2
!CamY       = $0A       ; 2
!InputPressed = $0C     ; 2 - what the pose machine acts on: see !PadHeld
!ArgClass   = $0E
!ArgId      = $0F
!Blob       = $10       ; 3
!BlobLen    = $13       ; 2
!Dir        = $15       ; 3
!DirCount   = $18       ; 2
!Script     = $1A       ; 3
!Src        = $1D       ; 3
!Meta       = $20       ; 3
!Body       = $23       ; 3
!BodyIdx    = $26       ; 2
!Col        = $28       ; 2
!Row        = $2A       ; 2
!Dest       = $2C       ; 2
!Tmp        = $2E       ; 2
!Op         = $30
!Words      = $31       ; 2
!Skip       = $33       ; 2
!Slot       = $35       ; 2
!StreamDir  = $37       ; which directions owe a row or column
!StreamX    = $38       ; 2
!StreamY    = $3A       ; 2
!StreamN    = $3C       ; 2
!StreamAxis = $3E       ; 2 - 0 walks the row rightwards, 1 walks the column down
!StreamCell = $40       ; 2 - the cell !StreamBody was resolved from, or $FFFF
!StreamBody = $42       ; 3 - bank byte 0 means that cell has no screen
!StreamDest = $45       ; 2
; The input pair the original keeps in HRAM as `hInputPressed` /
; `hInputRisingEdge`. `!PadTmp` is `ReadPad`'s own scratch and not shared with
; anything: the read runs inside NMI, so a variable it borrowed from the main
; loop would be clobbered underneath whatever was interrupted.
!InputRisingEdge = $48  ; 2
!PadTmp          = $4A  ; 2

; Samus. A position is `screen<<8 | pixel`, the same packing the camera uses and
; the same the Game Boy keeps in two HRAM bytes.
!SamusX     = $4C       ; 2
!SamusY     = $4E       ; 2
!PrevX      = $50       ; 2 - where the frame began, which is what a failed
!PrevY      = $52       ; 2   move reverts to
!Pose       = $54
!Facing     = $55
!AirDir     = $56       ; latched at a spin jump's launch, not steered
!JumpArc    = $57
!FallArc    = $58
!JumpStart  = $59
!TurnTimer  = $5A
!Items      = $5B       ; zero in Phase 0a; the branches exist anyway
!Water      = $5C       ; per-frame, latched by the collision routines
!Unhandled  = $5D       ; the first pose Phase 0a was handed and could not run
!Solid      = $5E       ; samusSolidityIndex: ids below it are solid to Samus
!Block      = $5F       ; the block-type byte the last SampleTile read
!Hit        = $60       ; and whether that tile was solid
!ColTab     = $61       ; 3 - the tileset's block-type table
!FallArcP   = $64       ; 3 - the three arcs and the two hitbox tables, resolved
!JumpArcP   = $67       ; 3   once at boot rather than searched for per frame
!SpaceArcP  = $6A       ; 3
!HitboxP    = $6D       ; 3
!YOffsP     = $70       ; 3
!JumpArcLen = $73       ; 2 - what the dropped $80 terminator became
!TileX      = $75       ; 2
!TileY      = $77       ; 2
!SpeedR     = $79       ; the four camera speeds Samus's movement sets
!SpeedL     = $7A
!SpeedU     = $7B
!SpeedD     = $7C
!PrevYPix   = $7D       ; last frame's pixel row, for the vertical camera delta
!ColTmp     = $7E       ; 2 - SampleTile's own
!Offs       = $80       ; 10 - CollideHoriz's gathered offsets, one word each
!MoveB      = $8A       ; 2 - the movers' speed, theirs
!PoseTmp    = $8C       ; 2 - the pose machine's own
!Guide      = $8E       ; 2 - the camera's, and !CamTmp with it
!CamTmp     = $90       ; 2

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The variables the oracle compares, exported as labels.
;
; `!SamusX` and the rest are assembler defines, and a define leaves no trace in
; engine.sym -- so `src/correspond.zig`, which pairs each Game Boy address with
; ours out of that file, could not see them. These assignments put them there.
; They assemble to nothing; asar emits a symbol and no bytes.
;
; Named `Var*` rather than reusing the define's name so that a symbol lookup
; can never be satisfied by a routine label that happens to match.
;
; The first eight are what the oracle compares. The rest are what
; `src/snes_trace.zig` records every frame once the oracle has said a frame
; disagrees: the comparator names *when*, and a run of these names *why* -- the
; input the engine saw, the pose it dispatched on, the position it reverted to,
; and the tile the collision sampled. Exported for the same reason as the
; first eight and at the same cost, which is none: asar emits a symbol and no
; bytes.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
VarSamusX   = !SamusX
VarSamusY   = !SamusY
VarPose     = !Pose
VarFacing   = !Facing
VarCamX     = !CamX
VarCamY     = !CamY
VarMapIndex = !MapIndex
VarCell     = !Cell
VarScroll   = !Scroll

VarFrameCount      = !FrameCount
VarInputPressed    = !InputPressed
VarInputRisingEdge = !InputRisingEdge
VarPrevX           = !PrevX
VarPrevY           = !PrevY
VarJumpArc         = !JumpArc
VarFallArc         = !FallArc
VarTurnTimer       = !TurnTimer
VarWater           = !Water
VarUnhandled       = !Unhandled
VarSolid           = !Solid
VarColTab          = !ColTab
VarBlock           = !Block
VarHit             = !Hit
VarSpeedR          = !SpeedR
VarSpeedL          = !SpeedL
VarMoveB           = !MoveB
VarTileX           = !TileX
VarTileY           = !TileY

; The four sprite ids the ball and the knockback draw with, exported so
; `src/sprites.zig` can check them against the tables at 01:$4C69 and 01:$4CB5 -
; which Step 11 pinned, and which until then were transcriptions nothing graded.
; A transcription that is checked against the cartridge is a derivation; one
; that is not is a number someone typed. asar emits a symbol and no bytes.
; The six equipment masks, exported so `correspond.zig` can compare them
; against the bit each pickup arm actually sets in the cartridge. See
; `docs/bug_tracker.md`, 2026-09-09: two of them were wrong.
ConstItemBomb   = !ITEM_BOMB
ConstItemHiJump = !ITEM_HIJUMP
ConstItemScrew  = !ITEM_SCREW
ConstItemSpace  = !ITEM_SPACE
ConstItemSpring = !ITEM_SPRING
ConstItemSpider = !ITEM_SPIDER
ConstItemVaria  = !ITEM_VARIA

ConstSprKnockL = !SPR_KNOCK_L
ConstSprKnockR = !SPR_KNOCK_R
ConstSprBallL  = !SPR_BALL_L
ConstSprBallR  = !SPR_BALL_R
ConstPoseMorphBombed = !POSE_MORPHBOMBED
ConstPoseSpiderRoll = !POSE_SPIDERROLL
ConstPoseSpiderFall = !POSE_SPIDERFALL
ConstPoseSpiderJump = !POSE_SPIDERJUMP
ConstPoseSpider     = !POSE_SPIDER
ConstPoseRun        = !POSE_RUN
ConstSprSpiderL = !SPR_SPIDER_L
ConstSprSpiderR = !SPR_SPIDER_R

; The spider ball's probe offsets and table rows, for `correspond.zig`.
ConstSpiderXLeft   = !SPIDER_X_LEFT
ConstSpiderXRight  = !SPIDER_X_RIGHT
ConstSpiderXMid    = !SPIDER_X_MID
ConstSpiderYTop    = !SPIDER_Y_TOP
ConstSpiderYBottom = !SPIDER_Y_BOTTOM
ConstSpiderYMid    = !SPIDER_Y_MID
ConstSpiderTry2    = !SPIDER_TRY2
ConstSpiderCw      = !SPIDER_CW

; The hitbox offsets, exported the same way and for a reader rather than for the
; engine. `snes_trace.zig` reproduces `CollideBottom`'s probe to say where the
; floor is under Samus, and a copy of these numbers in Zig would be a second
; place for them to be wrong. asar emits a symbol whose value is the constant.
ConstOriginXLeft   = !ORIGIN_X_LEFT
ConstOriginXRight  = !ORIGIN_X_RIGHT
ConstOriginYBottom = !ORIGIN_Y_BOTTOM

; Drawing Samus. `!SpriteId`, `!SprX` and `!SprY` are the original's
; `hSpriteId`, `hSpriteXPixel` and `hSpriteYPixel`, and they carry Game Boy OAM
; coordinates - the SNES ones are produced at the shadow write and nowhere else.
!SpriteId   = $92
!AnimTimer  = $93       ; $D022 samus_animationTimer: the run cycle, and a crossing's camera adds 3
VarAnimTimer = !AnimTimer   ; exported for the trace; see the Var* block above
!SpinTimer  = $94       ; samus_spinAnimationTimer, incremented every frame
VarSpinTimer = !SpinTimer   ; exported for `snes boot` phase 27
!SprX       = $95       ; 2
!SprY       = $97       ; 2
!OamIdx     = $99       ; 2 - hOamBufferIndex, a byte index into the shadow
!OamMax     = $9B       ; 2 - maxOamPrevFrame: how far last frame got
!MsPtrs     = $9D       ; 3 - the converted pointer table
!MsData     = $A0       ; 3 - the metasprite records
!MsRec      = $A3       ; 3 - the record being walked
!PoseJumpP  = $A6       ; 3 - the four pose sprite-id tables, resolved at boot
!PoseSpinP  = $A9       ; 3
!PoseStandP = $AC       ; 3
!PoseRunP   = $AF       ; 3
!DrawTmp    = $B2       ; DrawSprite's own, and SamusSpriteId's
!DrawAttr   = $B3
!DrawX      = $B4       ; 2 - the ninth bit of which goes in the high table

; The pad as the *poll* left it, which is not the pad the pose machine acts on.
; The original polls after `samus_handlePose` has already run, so a button
; pressed during frame N first moves Samus on frame N+1; our NMI polls before
; the main loop resumes, which would have moved her on frame N. `!PadHeld` and
; `!PadEdge` are the original's $FF80/$FF81 - the poll's own latch, and the
; previous state its rising-edge XOR consumes - and `PublishPad` hands them to
; `!InputPressed`/`!InputRisingEdge` one frame later. Measured, not assumed:
; with the poll's value acted on the same frame the cart ran two pixels ahead
; of the reference from the first frame of every walk and stayed there.
!PadHeld    = $B8       ; 2
!PadEdge    = $BA       ; 2
; Exported for the frame trace, beside the `Var*` block above -- it cannot live
; there because the block is assembled before this define exists.
VarPadHeld  = !PadHeld

; How fast Samus was asked to fall on the last downward move that landed.
; `samus_moveDown` keeps it in $D034 for the duration of the move and copies
; it into $D033 only when the move succeeded, so a blocked fall leaves the
; previous frame's figure standing. The ball reads it to tell a landing from
; a roll: two or more pixels a frame and it bounces instead of rolling.
!DownSpeed  = $BC

; $D062, `acidContactFlag`, the other per-frame contact flag beside `!Water`.
; Every probe that samples an acid tile latches $40 into it -- the top and
; bottom probes and the spider's -- and `HandlePose` clears it at the top of
; every frame. **It was `!AcidContact` until Step 22**, read as a block that
; throws the ball back up; the $40 is M2RoS's "supposed to be a jump table
; index (behavior is unused)". Its readers: `DrawSamus` flickers her while it
; is set (01:$4BE6), and the ball's and the fall's A buttons take it as an arc
; index (00:$126D, $1301) -- which, per M2RoS, never fires, because both read
; it after `HandlePose` has cleared it and before any probe has run.
!AcidContact = $BD
VarAcidContact = !AcidContact

; $D049, counted down once a frame by the main loop at 00:$0556 and set to $10
; by an unmorph in mid-air. It is the window in which the aerial jump is
; allowed: `PoseFall` checks it before letting A start a jump out of a fall,
; which is what makes "unmorph and jump" work and an ordinary fall not.
!UnmorphGrace = $BE

; One-shot: set by `InitState`, cleared by the first pass of `MainLoop`, which
; is when `BootInput` is applied to `!InputPressed`.
;
; **It has to be applied there and not in `InitState`, and the difference is
; measurable.** `InitState` seeds `!PadHeld` and enabling NMI a dozen
; instructions later fires one immediately, whose `PublishPad` consumes the
; seed before the main loop has run a frame -- so the seed reached
; `!InputPressed` one frame early and the first graded frame saw zero. The
; frame trace shows exactly that: `held` holding the seed and `pressed` zero.
!InputSeeded = $BF

; ---- The room transition, mirrored from the original's door mechanism ------
; A transition is four things in sequence, and they are ported in the order the
; original writes them: `HandleCamera` notices Samus has walked into a blocked
; edge and calls `StartTransition` (00:$0C37), which turns the cell into a door
; index; `MainLoop` sees the index and runs `RunDoorScript` (00:$239C); the
; script's `WARP` (00:$28FB) re-seats the map bank, the cell, Samus and the
; camera. Step 5 of the Phase 0b slice. Streaming the incoming screen before
; control returns is Step 6, and until it lands the transition costs a black
; frame -- see `RunPendingTransition`.

; $D00E. Which of the four arrangements the transition uses. Read at 00:$08FE
; before anything else in the camera update and cleared at 00:$28E5 when the
; transition finishes, so zero means "no transition in progress" -- the state
; Phase 0a was permanently in.
;
; The four values are named from the arms they select in `handleWarp`, which is
; the only place their meaning is written down: $2939 draws the three columns at
; camera + $50/$60/$70, to the right; $29C4 at camera - $60/$70/$80, to the
; left; $2B04 three rows above; $2A4F three rows below. Each is reached from the
; edge of the same name in `handleCamera`.
!TransDir    = $C0
!TRANS_RIGHT = $01
!TRANS_LEFT  = $02
!TRANS_UP    = $04
!TRANS_DOWN  = $08

; $D08E/$D08F. The door script the pending transition will run, as an index into
; the converted pointer table. Nonzero means "a transition is owed", which is
; the test the original makes at 00:$01A0 and 00:$0568 ahead of everything else
; in a frame; the interpreter clears it on the way out at 00:$26DE.
!DoorIndex  = $C1       ; 2

; $D08B, the room mode. $11 is the Queen's room, and three branches read it: the
; downward trigger clamps at $A0 rather than $C0 (00:$0A50), the upward trigger
; sets Samus's position but does not warp (00:$0AE3), and `StartTransition`
; rewrites a spider-ball pose (00:$0C3E). All three were ported before anything
; could reach them, because a branch left out is a branch that silently does
; nothing the first time the slice grows into it; `ENTER_QUEEN` writes the $11
; since 1.0 Step 6. `WARP` masks it to a nibble at
; 00:$246B.
!RoomMode   = $C3

; What the four recording stubs saw. The original acts on each; the slice has
; none of the subsystems yet, so the opcode records its operand and stops. That
; is not the same as skipping by length: a recorded value is something a fixture
; can assert, and $FF is "this opcode has not run in this cart's lifetime".
!Song       = $C4       ; `currentRoomSong` ($D092): the song the room asks
                        ; for when what is playing is not it (00:$0EAF), what a
                        ; door's $Cx opcode writes, and what a Metroid's death
                        ; adds $11 to (02:$4051). A save-file value, seeded from
                        ; `BootRoomSong` -- it booted at $FF until Step 18, and
                        ; a new game had no music at all for it.
!AcidDmg    = $C5       ; $6x, first operand ($D077)
!SpikeDmg   = $C6       ; $6x, second ($D078)
!ItemGiven  = $C7       ; $Dx
!MetLess    = $C8       ; $9x, the Metroid count it compares against
!MetLessTo  = $C9       ; 2 - and the script it runs when taken. Step 14.
!WarpTmp    = $CB       ; 2 - the warp handler's own
!WarpRow    = $CD
!WarpCol    = $CE

; ---- The transition's duration --------------------------------------------
; The original's interpreter is frame-paced: it waits a frame on entry
; (00:$23AF) and a frame after every opcode (00:$26D1, which every dispatch arm
; jumps to), and three opcodes wait far longer than that because the Game Boy
; can only move 64 bytes into VRAM per vblank. The port cannot block -- there is
; no `HALT` to return from -- so the interpreter is turned inside out: one
; opcode per frame, driven from `MainLoop` by `RunPendingTransition`, with the
; opcode's remaining cost in `!TransWait`. The frame counts that fall out are
; the ones `src/transition.zig` grades against the Game Boy, opcode for opcode,
; on every door script in the ROM.
!TransPtr   = $CF       ; 3 - the script, kept across the frames it takes
!TransY     = $D2       ; 2 - how far into it the interpreter has got
!TransWait  = $D4       ; 2 - frames still owed on the opcode just run
!TransRun   = $D6       ; the script has been started; zero is the entry wait
!TransCls   = $D7       ; the CopyClass `DoCopy` is about to consume
!TransTmp   = $D8       ; 2 - `TransitionCamera`'s own
!SeedX      = $DA       ; 2 - `SeedWindow`'s column walk, and its own
!SeedN      = $DC       ; 2

; ---- The appearance sequence ----------------------------------------------
; $D066/$D067, the original's `countdownTimerLow`/`countdownTimerHigh`. One
; sixteen-bit counter, decremented once a frame by the vblank handler at
; 00:$0165 and floored at zero there rather than allowed to wrap. `loadGame_
; samusData` (00:$0CA3) sets it to $0140 as the last thing a load does, and
; pose $13 waits on it: 320 frames of Samus standing in front of the camera
; before the game hands over control. That is trace frames 6 to 325 of the any%
; run exactly, and it is what stands between the cart and the movie's frame 0.
;
; The original calls it a generic countdown and uses it for several unrelated
; events; ported here as the one variable it is, so the sequences that arrive
; later share it the way the original shares it.
!Countdown  = $DE       ; 2

; $D079, `loadingFromFile`. Zero for a new game, $FF for a load, and pose $13
; reads it to decide whether the player has to press a button: a new game waits
; for one, a loaded file does not. **Nothing writes it yet.** Every path into
; the cart today is a new game, and the writer arrives with B7 in Step 15 -- the
; branch is ported now because a branch left out is a branch that silently does
; nothing the first time the slice grows into it.
!LoadingFromFile = $E0

; Whether the pose's draw routine declined this frame. `drawSamus_faceScreen`
; (01:4D33) returns before it names a sprite on one frame in four while the
; countdown's low byte is nonzero, which is the flicker Samus appears out of --
; so the skip has to reach past `SamusSpriteId` and stop the draw, not just
; leave `!SpriteId` alone.
!SprSkip    = $E1

; `TitleScreen`'s running VRAM destination, in words. Its own, and not `!Tmp`:
; the four character blobs are laid down across four calls to `FindBlob`, and
; borrowing a variable across a call is the shape of the bug that once made
; every character copy run with the directory search's loop counter as its
; length. See the scratch-ownership note below.
!TitleDst   = $E2       ; 2

; The enemy class's four blobs, resolved once at boot. **In the direct page and
; not beside the rest of the entity variables**, and the reason is an addressing
; mode: `lda [dp],y` is the only way to read through a 24-bit pointer with an
; index, and its pointer operand has to be a direct-page address. The variables
; the entity pass merely stores in live at $0100; these four are dereferenced.
!EnData     = $E4       ; 3 - the per-screen spawn lists
!EnPtrs     = $E7       ; 3 - one byte offset into them per screen
!EnHdrs     = $EA       ; 3 - the 11-byte enemy headers
!EnPtrs2    = $ED       ; 3 - one byte offset into those per enemy id
; B4b's three, dereferenced in the per-slot collision loop for the same reason
; the four above are: `lda [dp],y` and no other addressing mode reaches a
; runtime 24-bit base with an index.
!EnDmg      = $F0       ; 3 - one damage byte per enemy id
!EnHbox     = $F3       ; 3 - the four-signed-byte hitbox records
!EnXPtrs    = $F6       ; 3 - one byte offset into those per enemy id
; The shared dereference slot for the four *Samus* tables B4b adds. **Not
; borrowed scratch**: it has one owner per use and the value is loaded from
; `!BombArcA` and its three neighbours immediately before the read, never
; carried across a call. The direct page is out of room for four more pointers
; and these four are read once per event where the enemy tables are read once
; per slot per frame, so this is where the cost was cheapest to pay.
!TabP       = $F9       ; 3

; $D08A, `beamSolidityIndex`: the *third* threshold the `SOLIDITY` op leaves,
; and the one the beam's block classification compares a tile id against. The
; op reads three bytes out of one row and stores them in order -- 00:$2447 to
; `$D056` (Samus's, the port's `!Solid`), 00:$244E to `$D069` (the enemies'),
; 00:$2455 to `$D08A` (this one) -- so **the ROM states which column is which**,
; and `src/blocks.zig` reads the column index out of that store sequence rather
; than out of a comment. The enemies' threshold has no reader on this cart and
; so has no variable, per `residue.zig`'s rule.
;
; Here rather than beside `!Solid` because $5B-$63 is full; the residue scan
; reads the source, not the address.
!SolidBeam  = $FC

; Step 24i. The selected slot's record or spawn-flag window in cartridge RAM,
; as a long pointer: `SlotRecP` and `SlotSpawnP` set it, and the save, the load
; and the title read through it with `[dp],y`. The Game Boy's is DE or HL.
!SlotP      = $FD       ; 3

; Where the scroll stops, one per direction: the camera's pixel byte is compared
; against these and an exact match ends the crossing. 00:$0B7C, $0BB0, $0BE9 and
; $0C21. Three of the four are camera clamps the port already names; the fourth
; is not `!CAM_MAX_Y`, and the eight pixels between them are the original's, not
; a slip -- an upward crossing lands eight pixels above where a downward scroll
; would have been clamped.
!TRANS_STOP_RIGHT = !CAM_MIN_X          ; $50
!TRANS_STOP_LEFT  = !CAM_MAX_X          ; $B0
!TRANS_STOP_UP    = $B8
!TRANS_STOP_DOWN  = !CAM_MIN_Y          ; $48

; Exported for the frame trace and the residue audit, beside the `Var*` block
; above -- they cannot live there because the block is assembled before these
; defines exist, which is the same reason `VarPadHeld` is down here.
VarTransDir  = !TransDir
VarTransWait = !TransWait
VarDoorIndex = !DoorIndex
VarRoomMode  = !RoomMode
VarSong      = !Song
VarItemGiven = !ItemGiven

; Exported for the cold-boot rung, which watches the appearance sequence tick
; down. Down here for the same reason as the block above: the `Var*` block near
; the top is assembled before `!Countdown` exists.
VarCountdown = !Countdown
VarOamIdx    = !OamIdx

; Scratch is owned, not shared. `!Dir`, `!DirCount`, `!Blob` and `!BlobLen`
; belong to `FindBlob`; `!Src` to `BlobOffset` and `MetaOffset`; `!Words` to
; `DoCopy`; `!Skip` to the script walker; `!Slot` to `LoadScreen`; everything
; named `!Stream*` to the streamer. One variable
; served three of those once, and `DoCopy` held a transfer length in it across a
; call to `FindBlob` that used it as a loop counter - so every character copy
; ran with a length of whatever the directory search had counted down to, and
; the cart drew a screen of transparent tiles over a black backdrop.

!TilemapBuf   = $0800
!TilemapBytes = 32*32*2
; Not a variable but the buffer collision reads: `snes_trace.zig` copies it out
; whole, so the world our engine walks through can be diffed against the world
; the reference walked through.
VarTilemapBuf      = !TilemapBuf

; The OAM shadow: the 512-byte low table and the 32-byte high table, contiguous,
; so one DMA fills both - OAM's address counter runs straight from one into the
; other. It sits above the tilemap buffer and below the stack.
!OamBuf       = $1000
!OamLowBytes  = 512
!OamHigh      = !OamBuf+!OamLowBytes
!OamBufBytes  = !OamLowBytes+32
!OamSlots     = 128
; Where an unused slot is parked. The original writes zero, and zero on a Game
; Boy is sixteen pixels above the screen and so invisible; zero on a SNES is the
; top-left of a visible frame. The play window's HDMA band happens to cover that
; corner today, but that is a property of where the window sits rather than of
; the slot being unused, so the hidden row is written explicitly.
!OAM_HIDDEN_Y = $F0
; Exported so the gate can assert a slot the frame did not use is *parked*
; rather than left holding last frame's picture. Step 12d's, and it is the
; invariant `ClearUnusedOam` exists for -- see `DrawSamus` for what moving that
; call out of it was about.
ConstOamHiddenY = !OAM_HIDDEN_Y


; ---- Entities, mirrored from M2RoS SRC/ram/wram.asm ------------------------
; The original keeps three arrays and a working copy: sixteen $20-byte slots at
; $C600, a 128-byte spawn-flag array at $C500, and an HRAM mirror at $FFE0 that
; every per-enemy routine actually operates on. The port keeps the two arrays
; and **not the mirror**: the mirror exists because the Game Boy's `ld [hl+]`
; through HRAM is a byte cheaper than through WRAM, and a 65816 with a slot
; address in a direct-page pointer pays nothing to work in place. What that
; costs is that the field offsets differ between the two -- HRAM has
; `spawnFlag`/`spawnNumber`/`pAI` at $FFEF-$FFF2 and the screen bytes after
; them, WRAM has the screen bytes at +$0F/+$10 and the flag block at +$1C -- so
; every routine below cites the *WRAM* offset even where the original's source
; names an `hEnemy` field.
;
; They live above the OAM shadow rather than in the direct page: 512 bytes of
; slots plus 128 of flags is more than twice what is left of $00xx, and nothing
; here is read often enough for the direct page's one saved byte per access to
; matter. $0100-$07FF was unused and is where they go.
!Slots        = $0200           ; $C600. 16 x $20
!SlotSize     = $20
!SlotCount    = 16
!SlotsEnd     = !Slots+!SlotCount*!SlotSize

; Slot fields, from `enemyDataSlots`' own comment in wram.asm. The nine header
; bytes at +$04 are copied out of `enemy_headers` verbatim by `LoadOneEnemy`;
; only the ones a ported routine reads are named.
!EN_STATUS    = $00             ; $00 active, $01 offscreen, $FF empty
!EN_YPOS      = $01             ; camera-space pixel, Game Boy OAM biased
!EN_XPOS      = $02
!EN_SPRITE    = $03             ; the enemy id: header, damage and hitbox share it
!EN_HEADER    = $04             ; nine bytes, +$04 to +$0C
; The header's interior, named in B4b because the AI reads it. From `hEnemy`'s
; own field list in hram.asm: the mirror at $FFE0 is the slot byte for byte, so
; an `hEnemy.counter` in the original's source is +$09 here and nothing else.
!EN_BASEATTR  = $04             ; the header's first byte, never modified
!EN_ATTR      = $05             ; $20 X flip, $40 Y flip; the AI writes it
!EN_STUN      = $06             ; $00 idle, $10 frozen palette, $11-$13 stunned
!EN_GENVAR    = $07             ; a counter or a state, per AI
!EN_DIRFLAGS  = $08             ; direction, and the vulnerability nibble above it
!EN_COUNTER   = $09             ; the behaviour counter, per AI
!EN_STATE     = $0A             ; the behaviour state, per AI
!EN_ICE       = $0B             ; the freeze counter
!EN_HEALTH    = $0C             ; the ninth, which is also the initial health
!EN_DROP      = $0D             ; cleared on load, with the three below it
!EN_EXPLODE   = $0E
!EN_YSCREEN   = $0F             ; how many screens away, signed as $FF/$00/$01
!EN_XSCREEN   = $10
!EN_MAXHP     = $11
!EN_FLAG      = $1C             ; this slot's copy of its `!SpawnFlags` byte
!EN_NUMBER    = $1D             ; which byte of `!SpawnFlags` that is
!EN_AI        = $1E             ; 2 - the AI pointer. B4b's; loaded, never called

; $C500. Two $40-byte halves: the low one is per-room scratch the original fills
; with $FF at 02:$418C, the high one is the *saved* half, copied in and out of
; the save bank. Nothing here saves it -- that is B7's -- so both halves are
; filled with $FF at boot and the row in `residue.zig` says so.
;
; A spawn number indexes the whole 128 as one array: the ROM's own range is $0B
; to $7B, which straddles the halves, and the original's `ld d, HIGH(...)` makes
; no distinction either.
!SpawnFlags   = $0400
!SpawnFlagBytes = $80
!FLAG_NEW     = $FF             ; never loaded
!FLAG_SEEN    = $FE             ; loaded once, gone again, may reload
!FLAG_ACTIVE  = $01             ; live, and new
!FLAG_DEAD    = $02
!FLAG_WAITING = $03
!FLAG_ACTIVE_SEEN = $04         ; live, and had been seen before
!FLAG_SEEN_FIRED  = $05
!FLAG_CHILD   = $06             ; a projectile, deleted rather than deactivated

; The enemy pass's own variables. Absolute rather than direct page for the same
; reason the arrays are: the direct page is full, and none of these is read more
; than a few times a frame.
;
; `!EnTop*` through `!EnRight*` are the original's `topEdge_pixel` and its seven
; neighbours -- the camera's four edges, each as a screen number and a pixel
; within it, rounded down to a block or a tile. They are recomputed at the top
; of every `LoadEnemies` and read by both halves of the walk.
!EnTopPix     = $0100
!EnTopScr     = $0101
!EnBotPix     = $0102
!EnBotScr     = $0103
!EnLeftPix    = $0104
!EnLeftScr    = $0105
!EnRightPix   = $0106
!EnRightScr   = $0107
; `hTemp.a`: whichever of the four edge pixels this pass's walk is comparing
; against. The original reuses one HRAM byte for it and so does this.
!EnSeam       = $0108
; `loadEnemies_oscillator`. XOR'd with 1 every pass, and the walk goes
; horizontal on the frames it comes out zero. So each axis is checked every
; other frame, which is the original's whole answer to the cost of the walk.
!EnOsc        = $0109
!EnTotal      = $010A           ; numEnemies.total
!EnActive     = $010B           ; .active
!EnOffscr     = $010C           ; .offscreen
; `scrollHistory_B`, which only `LoadEnemies` reads: the scroll two frames ago,
; because a pass that runs on alternate frames has to compare against the last
; time *it* ran, not against the last frame.
!HistBY1      = $010D
!HistBY2      = $010E
!HistBX1      = $010F
!HistBX2      = $0110
; `scrollHistory_A`, which `ScrollEnemies` reads: the scroll one frame ago.
; The original keeps four entries; the two the port reads are the two it has.
!HistAY1      = $0111
!HistAX1      = $0112
; A byte offset into the spawn-list blob, not an address: the blob is 4452
; bytes and lives inside one bank, so `lda [!EnData],y` with this in Y reaches
; any of it and no pointer has to be carried alongside.
!EnList       = $0114           ; 2 - the cursor walking a screen's spawn list
!EnSlot       = $0116           ; 2 - the slot being filled
!EnTmp        = $0118           ; 4 - the walk's own scratch: the original's B,
                                ;     C and D, plus one the port needs because
                                ;     it re-indexes where the Game Boy walks a
                                ;     pointer
!EnDeltaY     = $011C           ; `ScrollEnemies`' dy and dx, as the original's
!EnDeltaX     = $011D           ; B and C
!EnFlagTmp    = $011E           ; `loadEnemy_spawnFlagTemp`
!EnMoved      = $011F           ; `hasMovedOffscreen`
!EnScrollY    = $0120           ; the Game Boy's `scrollY`, derived per pass
!EnScrollX    = $0121           ; and `scrollX`
!EnSwap       = $0122           ; `SwapNibbles`' own
!EnIdx        = $0123           ; 2 - `SpawnListAt`'s own
; The room reset a transition owes. 00:$0C83 writes $02 into `doorExitStatus`
; and the enemy pass is what services it, which is why `StartTransition` only
; sets a byte.
!SpawnReload  = $0126

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; B4b's own. The enemy pass's clock, Samus's contact state, and the scratch the
; one-enemy hitbox test works in.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; `hEnemy_frameCounter`. Incremented once per *pass*, not once per frame, and
; the AIs divide it further: the small bug's animation is `.now`, the Senjoo's
; is `and $01` on top of the pass's own alternation. So a Senjoo animates every
; four displayed frames and it is two halvings that make it four, not one.
!EnFrame      = $0127
; `enemy_sameEnemyFrameFlag` and `enemiesLeftToProcess`, and **these two are
; the 30 FPS gate, not a lag mechanism.** See `ProcessEnemies`.
!EnSame       = $0128
!EnLeftN      = $0129
; `samus_hurtFlag` ($C422), `samus_damageBoostDirection` ($C423) and
; `samus_damageValue` ($C424): what `collision_samusOneEnemy` leaves behind and
; `hurtSamus` reads on the *next* frame's top, because the original calls the
; two in that order and never the other way round.
!HurtFlag     = $012A
!BoostDir     = $012B
!DmgValue     = $012C
; `samusSpriteCollisionProcessedFlag` ($C425). Cleared by the play handler and
; set by the standard entry, so the loop runs once a frame however many callers
; reach it. The horizontal entry deliberately does not test it.
!SprCollDone  = $012D
; `collision_weaponType` and `collision_pEnemy`. The original stores a WRAM
; *address*; this stores the slot's byte offset, because that is what X holds
; here and an address would have to be converted back before it could be used.
!CollWeapon   = $012E
!CollEnemy    = $012F           ; 2
; `samusInvulnerableTimer` ($D04F) and `samusCurHealth` ($D051/$D052, BCD).
!Invuln       = $0131
VarInvuln     = !Invuln
!HealthLo     = $0132
!HealthHi     = $0133
; `hCollision_*`: the enemy's own four bytes, then the box the hitbox record
; and the two flips resolve to, then Samus's point. The original keeps all of
; it in HRAM and so does the port's arithmetic - as bytes, because every
; comparison in `collision_samusOneEnemy` is an 8-bit one and widening any of
; them would change which side of a wrap a value lands on.
!ColEnY       = $0134
!ColEnX       = $0135
!ColEnSpr     = $0136
!ColEnAttr    = $0137
!ColEnIce     = $0138
!ColTop       = $0139
!ColBot       = $013A
!ColLeft      = $013B
!ColRight     = $013C
!ColSamY      = $013D           ; `hTemp.a`, computed fresh every frame
!ColSamX      = $013E           ; `hTemp.b`, which is *last* frame's draw
!ColB         = $013F           ; the routine's B, C and D
!ColC         = $0140
!ColD         = $0141
; The four Samus tables B4b adds, resolved once at boot. They live here rather
; than in the direct page because the direct page has sixteen bytes left and
; the enemy tables - read in the per-slot loop - are the ones that need them.
; Each reader copies the one it wants into `!TabP` first; see `!TabP`.
!BombArcA     = $0142           ; 3
!BombArcLen   = $0145           ; 2 - what the dropped $80 became
!SprHbA       = $0147           ; 3
!DmgPoseA     = $014A           ; 3
!BombPoseA    = $014D           ; 3
; The vertical entries' overlap, which `CollideSamusEnemiesDown` lifts Samus out
; by (`hTemp.c`), and whether the entry running is a vertical one.
!ColDist      = $0150
!ColVert      = $0151
; `samus_onSolidSprite` ($C43A, 00:$1F19). Written by the two vertical loops and
; by `CollideBottom`; its readers are the spider ball's, and since Step 14b they
; are here: a floor that is an enemy is not snapped to (00:$115E, 00:$1233).
!OnSolidSprite = $0152
; The first enemy state `EnemyCommonAI` was handed that this cart has no handler
; for, as the slot offset of the byte that was set, and the first AI pointer the
; dispatch table does not know. Both are $00 until one happens, the way
; `!Unhandled` is - a number the gate can print instead of a silent wrong turn.
!EnUnhandledState = $0153
!EnUnhandledAi    = $0154        ; 2
; Where `EnemyCommonAI` puts the routine address it found, because `jmp (abs)`
; takes an address and not a register.
!EnAiTarget       = $0156        ; 2
; Nonzero if `!FrameCount` was not `BootFrameCount + 1` on the first frame of
; `MainLoop`. **The one frame that can see it**: the seed is `InitState`'s and
; the increments are NMI's, so nothing outside the engine knows how many of the
; latter happened between them. The gate reads this byte and refuses the cart.
; See `bug_tracker.md`, 2026-09-09.
!FramePhase       = $0158

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; B6: the item pickup. Step 11.
;
; **The `ITEM` door opcode gives nothing**, and the plan's sub-task read as
; though it did. 00:$2634's arm loads four graphics blobs - the item's four
; tiles, the orb, the item font and the name out of `item_names` - and walks on;
; `!ItemGiven` is a record of a room having been *decorated*. Every `samusItems`
; bit is set somewhere else entirely, by a mechanism with three parts:
;
;   `enAI_itemOrb` (02:$4DD3)   an enemy AI. Touching the orb turns it into the
;                               item; touching the item sets `itemCollected`
;   `handleItemPickup` (00:$372F)   the play handler's per-frame test, which
;                               fires once and then **blocks for hundreds of
;                               frames**
;   `handleItemPickup_end` (00:$3A01)   the two wait loops that spend them
;
; The blocking is the whole shape of the thing and it is measured, not
; inferred. Four frames of `waitOneFrame` before the item is even given, then
; the jingle's countdown, then a tail waiting for the orb to delete itself.
; From the B11 recording, stride 1, four pickups:
;
;   Bomb          frozen 44 325..44 682, 358 frames, `$D045` at 44 329
;   Missile Tank  frozen 44 964..45 066, 103 frames, `$D081` at 44 968
;   Energy Tank   frozen 48 043..48 408, 366 frames, `$D050` at 48 047
;   Spider Ball   frozen 68 449..68 807, 359 frames, `$D045` at 68 453
;
; **The item lands exactly four frames after the freeze begins, in all four**,
; which is the four `waitOneFrame` calls and nothing else. The body is $0160
; frames for the three major items and $0060 for the Missile Tank, which is
; where 00:$375C's `cp $0D` partitions the item space. The tails are 2, 3, 10
; and 3 frames and are **not modelled as a constant**: the second loop runs
; until the orb's own AI deletes it, which depends on how many live slots the
; enemy pass walks and on its 30 Hz alternation - and the Energy Tank's ten is
; as likely to be James not pressing anything for seven more frames.
;
; The port cannot block, so the routine is turned inside out the way the door
; interpreter is: `!ItemStage` is where it got to and `MainLoop` drives it.
!ItemStage    = $0159
!ITEM_IDLE    = $00
!ITEM_WAIT4   = $01             ; the four `waitOneFrame`s: nothing is drawn
!ITEM_JINGLE  = $02             ; `handleItemPickup_end`'s first loop
!ITEM_DELETE  = $03             ; and its second, waiting on the orb
!ITEM_XFER    = $04             ; 1.0 Step 8a: the arm's `loadGraphics` waits
; 1.0 Step 9: the first loop's first pass, which does not test the countdown,
; and Varia's arm, which blocks three more times (`VariaStage`).
!ITEM_JINGLE1 = $05
!ITEM_VARIA_WAIT = $06          ; the fanfare's loop, 00:$38B9
!ITEM_VARIA_SFX  = $07          ; the sound, and `animateGettingVaria`'s first frame
!ITEM_VARIA_ANIM = $08          ; and its loop, 00:$27FA
; 1.0 Step 10: the missile refill with every Metroid dead. The original sets
; game mode $12 and returns, and never comes back to play. Since 1.0 Step 22
; this is the frame it returns into: the rest of the play handler, and then
; `!DeathMode` is $12 (`CreditsFrame`).
!ITEM_CREDITS = $09
!MODE_PREPARE_CREDITS = $12     ; 00:$39AC `LD A,$12`
!MODE_CREDITS = $13             ; 05:$5938, `prepareCredits`' `INC A`
!ItemFrames   = $015A           ; how many of the four waits are left

; `enSprCollision` ($C466-$C468), which is *not* `collision_weaponType`
; ($D05D-$D05F, the port's `!CollWeapon`/`!CollEnemy`). The second is what the
; hitbox test writes; the first is a copy of it taken at the enemy handler's
; own exit (02:$4318 `.transferCollisionResults`), so an AI reading it is
; reading the frame before's contact. That one-frame gap is the original's and
; is the same shape as `!HurtFlag`'s.
!SprWeapon    = $015B
!SprEnemy     = $015C           ; 2 - the slot offset, as `!CollEnemy` keeps it

!ItemCollected = $015E          ; $D06C, and the trigger: nonzero starts it all
!ItemCopy     = $015F           ; $D093, the working copy the loops read
!ItemFlag     = $0160           ; $D06D. $FF collecting, $03 done, $00 deleted
!ItemOrbWeapon = $0161          ; $D06F, saved so the loops can put it back
!ItemOrbEnemy = $0162           ; 2 - $D070/$D071, again as a slot offset
!ItemSprite   = $0164           ; $C388 `temp_spriteType`
!ItemOrbY     = $0165           ; $D094/$D095, which the original itself calls
!ItemOrbX     = $0166           ;   unused - ported because they are two stores
!ItemTmpC     = $0167           ; the AI's C: the sprite type it came in with
!WinY         = $0168           ; `rWY`: $88, or $80 while the window is raised (Step 24g)

; Samus's counters, which no step before this one needed. All four BCD, as the
; original stores them and as `save.fields` reads them back.
!Tanks        = $0169           ; $D050 `samusEnergyTanks`
!MaxMissLo    = $016A           ; $D081/$D082 `samusMaxMissiles`
!MaxMissHi    = $016B
!CurMissLo    = $016C           ; $D053/$D054 `samusCurMissiles`
!CurMissHi    = $016D
!Beam         = $016E           ; $D055 `samusBeam`
!ActiveWeapon = $016F           ; $D04D `samusActiveWeapon`

; The recording stubs this mechanism needs, in the shape `!Song` already has:
; the original acts on each, the port has no subsystem, so the value is stored
; where a fixture can assert it. ($0170 was `!GfxWanted`, the graphics a pickup
; asked for, until 1.0 Step 8a ported `loadGraphics`.)
!SongInt      = $0171           ; $CEDE `songInterruptionRequest`
!SongPlaying  = $0172           ; $CEDF `songInterruptionPlaying`
!Sfx1         = $0173           ; $CEC0 `sfxRequest_square1`
!SfxNoise     = $0174           ; $CED5 `sfxRequest_noise`
; The branch `pickup_missileRefill` takes when every Metroid is dead recorded
; the item that reached it here from 1.0 Step 10, while mode $12 was not
; ported. Since 1.0 Step 22 it is (`CreditsFrame`) and nothing records into
; this; the debug screen's status line still shows it, as zero.
!ItemUnhandled = $0175

; `enemy_weaponType` ($C46D), which `enemy_getSamusCollisionResults` leaves for
; the AI that called it. $FF is "not this enemy, or nothing touched anything".
!EnWeapon     = $0176
; The AI's own scratch, owned rather than borrowed - see the ownership note by
; `!Dir`. `!ItemTmpC2` is the original's second use of C in `.checkIfDone`, and
; `!ItemTmpW` is the word `EnemyCollisionResults` compares X against.
!ItemTmpC2    = $0177
!ItemTmpW     = $0178           ; 2

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; B5's terrain half: the destructible blocks. Step 12a.
;
; The original keeps sixteen $10-byte slots at $D900 and uses three bytes of
; each: a counter, and the block's Y and X. The port keeps the stride, because
; the eviction walks the array with `LD A,L / AND $F0 / ADD A,$10` and that
; arithmetic *is* the array -- a compacted three-byte slot would be a different
; routine wearing the same address comments.
;
; **The counter is the whole mechanism.** `destroyRespawningBlock` (01:$5671)
; writes $01; `handleRespawningBlocks` (01:$5692) increments it once a frame
; and dispatches on six exact values -- $02 and $07 crack, $0D empties, $F6 and
; $FA crack back the other way, $FE reforms and frees the slot. Nothing
; measures elapsed time; a byte wrapping through 256 frames is the timer, which
; is why the two halves of the animation are symmetric about the wrap.
;
; The array lives above `!SpawnFlags` and below `!TilemapBuf`, in the same
; $0100-$07FF window the entity arrays took and for the same reason.
!Blocks       = $0500           ; $D900. 16 x $10
!BLK_SIZE     = $10
!BLK_COUNT    = 16
!BLK_COUNTER  = $00             ; $00 frees the slot; $01 is a fresh block
!BLK_Y        = $01             ; the block's world pixel row, OAM biased
!BLK_X        = $02             ; and its column
; The six counter values `handleRespawningBlocks` dispatches on, and the four
; tile ids the arms write. Both sets are immediates in the ROM -- `CP $xx` at
; 01:$56BC and its five neighbours, `LD A,$xx` at 01:$5705, $575E and $5785 --
; and `src/blocks.zig` reads all ten out of those opcodes, so the block below
; is checked rather than transcribed. See `src/correspond.zig`.
!BLK_CRACK1   = $02
!BLK_CRACK2   = $07
!BLK_EMPTY_AT = $0D
!BLK_CRACK3   = $F6
!BLK_CRACK4   = $FA
!BLK_REFORM   = $FE
!BLK_TILE_SOLID = $00           ; $00-$03, the 2x2 a respawning block is
!BLK_TILE_A   = $04             ; $04-$07, the first crack
!BLK_TILE_B   = $08             ; $08-$0B, the second
!BLK_TILE_GONE = $FF            ; and the empty
; The two eviction bands. A slot whose distance from the scroll has high nibble
; $C vertically or $D horizontally is off the screen for good and is freed --
; 01:$56A6 and 01:$56B5, and they are bands rather than ranges because the
; original masks with $F0 before comparing.
!BLK_EVICT_Y  = $C0
!BLK_EVICT_X  = $D0
; The tile ids below `beamSolidityIndex` that are respawning blocks whatever
; their collision byte says: 01:$5168's `CP $04`.
!BLK_RESPAWN_IDS = $04

; The tilemap slot a draw is about to write, which is the original's $C215/$C216
; pair with the `AND $DE` already applied. There is no port of the slot pointer
; the original keeps in HL: an index register holds it here, which is why the
; array's stride is arithmetic in `HandleRespawningBlocks` and not a variable.
!BlkDst       = $017A           ; 2 - a tile index into !TilemapBuf, snapped
; **01:$5790 is recorded rather than ported**, and this byte is the recording.
; The boundary is a real one: the routine's first read is a nineteen-byte table
; at 01:$57DF, indexed by Samus's pose, that this repository has not pinned, and
; what it does with it is apply a knockback -- `samus_hurtFlag`,
; `samus_damageBoostDirection` and `samus_damageValue`, which is B5's other half
; and Step 12b's. Porting it blind would mean transcribing a ROM table into this
; file, which is what the file policy exists to stop.
;
; **What it records is 01:$5739's test and not 01:$5790's**, and the difference
; is worth being exact about: the gate at $5739 is `samusInvulnerableTimer` and
; nothing else, so this byte says "a block reformed while Samus was vulnerable",
; not "a block reformed on top of her". The overlap test -- her pose's height
; against the block's row, and $18 across -- lives inside $5790 with the table,
; and half-porting a geometry test would be worse than not porting it. $00 means
; the branch has never been reached, the way `!ItemUnhandled` does.
!BlkCrush     = $017C

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; B5's projectile half. Step 12b.
;
; The original keeps three $10-byte slots at $DD00 and uses six bytes of each.
; The stride is kept for the reason the block array's is: `getFirstEmptyProjectile
; Slot` walks the array with `LD A,L / SWAP A` (01:$5005) and the spazer's split
; asks which slot it is in with `AND $F0` (01:$50A0), so the sixteen-byte stride
; *is* the array -- a compacted six-byte slot would be a different routine
; wearing the same address comments.
;
; **Slot 2 is the missile's.** 01:$4FF4 starts the search at slot 0 for a beam
; and at slot 2 for a missile, so a missile can never take a beam's slot and at
; most one is ever in the air. The spazer is the mirror image: 01:$4F6C loops
; `samusShoot` back into itself until the third slot is full, which is where its
; three beams come from.
;
; They live above the block array, in the same $0100-$07FF window the entity
; arrays took and for the same reason.
!Projs        = $0600           ; $DD00. 3 x $10
!PR_SIZE      = $10
!PR_COUNT     = 3
!PR_TYPE      = $00             ; $FF empty; otherwise the weapon type
!PR_DIR       = $01             ; $01 right, $02 left, $04 up, $08 down
!PR_Y         = $02             ; the world pixel row, in Samus's own space
!PR_X         = $03
!PR_WAVE      = $04             ; the wave beam's index into its speed table
!PR_FRAME     = $05             ; frames since it was fired
!PR_NONE      = $FF
; `samusActiveWeapon`'s space, which is also `weapon_damage`'s index. Read out
; of the compares that dispatch on them: 01:$503E ($02 wave), 01:$5043 ($03
; spazer), 01:$5047 ($08 missile), 01:$5228 ($04 plasma) and 01:$52C8 ($07, the
; bomb beam).
!WPN_NORMAL   = $00
!WPN_ICE      = $01
!WPN_WAVE     = $02
!WPN_SPAZER   = $03
!WPN_PLASMA   = $04
!WPN_BOMBBEAM = $07
!WPN_MISSILE  = $08
; The two `samus_possibleShotDirections` values that are not direction masks:
; 01:$4EB0's `AND A` and 01:$4EB2's `CP $80`.
!SHOT_NONE    = $00
!SHOT_BOMB    = $80
; 01:$4E9C's `CP $10`: how many frames a held fire button waits between shots.
!BEAM_COOLDOWN = $10
; Two different $04s, kept as two defines because they are two decisions.
; 01:$4EF1 and 01:$4F06 take the first off the two cannon offsets; 01:$528D and
; 01:$5295 put the second back on before the tile sample, which is what makes
; the shot collide at its middle rather than at its corner.
!SHOT_ORIGIN_BIAS = $04
!PR_HIT_BIAS  = $04
; 01:$509C's `CP $05`: the spazer's outer beams only spread for this many
; frames. 01:$50AB's `SUB $02` is how far each one goes per frame.
!SPAZER_SPREAD = $05
!SPAZER_SPLIT = $02
; The speeds, from the `ADD`/`SUB` immediates: 01:$521E is the $04 every beam
; moves at, 01:$522E the $02 the plasma adds on top, and 01:$5100 the $02 the
; wave moves at while its table carries it sideways.
!BEAM_SPD     = $04
!PLASMA_EXTRA = $02
!WAVE_SPD     = $02
; The window `drawProjectiles` keeps a projectile inside, and it is the window
; that *deletes* one rather than hiding it: 01:$535B's `CP $08` and its three
; neighbours at $5361, $5367 and $536D. This is the only despawn a beam has --
; nothing anywhere counts a projectile's frames down.
!PR_WIN_LEFT  = $08
!PR_WIN_RIGHT = $A4
!PR_WIN_TOP   = $0C
!PR_WIN_BOT   = $94
; 01:$534C's `LD A,$7E` and 01:$5355's `$7F`: one character for a beam flying
; horizontally and one for a beam flying up or down. 01:$5351's `AND $03` is
; what asks which.
!SPR_BEAM_H   = $7E
!SPR_BEAM_V   = $7F
!PR_DIR_HORIZ = $03
; The graphics `toggleMissiles` asks for: `gfxInfo_cannonBeam` (00:$2249) and
; `gfxInfo_cannonMissile` (00:$2242), which are `GfxInfo`'s first two rows.
!CANNON_BEAM    = $00
!CANNON_MISSILE = $01
; 02:$4257 and 02:$42DC's `CP $10`, and 02:$42D0/$42D4's `$A0`/`$D0`. The first
; is the screw attack's weapon type, which is also the boundary above which a
; contact is a touch rather than a shot; the other two bound the Metroid sprite
; ids, which `enemy_getDamagedOrGiveDrop` refuses to damage at all.
!WPN_SCREW    = $10
!SPR_METROID_LO = $A0
!SPR_METROID_HI = $D0
; 02:$4320's `CP $FE`: an enemy at or above this health is invulnerable and
; plinks. 02:$4335's `$11` is the stun a surviving enemy takes.
!EN_HP_INVULN = $FE
!EN_STUN_HIT  = $11

; ---- B4c's first half: the explosion, and what it leaves --------------------
; `enemy_getDamagedOrGiveDrop` composes the explosion flag out of two halves and
; `enemy_animateExplosion` takes it apart again: bit 5 picks which of the two
; sprite progressions runs, and the low nibble is what the corpse leaves behind.
; So the flag is a byte three routines agree about, and every number below is
; read out of the site that carries it -- see `src/correspond.zig`.
;
; 02:$56BF's `BIT 5,A`. Set is the screw attack's explosion ($20, written at
; 02:$4341) and clear is the ordinary one ($10, at 02:$434C).
!EN_EXP_BIG   = $20
; The two progressions' first sprite ids, from the `ADD A,d8` that builds each
; from the counter: 02:$56D5 for the screw attack's six frames and 02:$56E2 for
; the ordinary three or four.
!SPR_EXP_BIG  = $E2
!SPR_EXP_NORM = $E8
; How many frames each runs. 02:$56D1's `CP $06` bounds the big one; the
; ordinary one is 02:$56C3's `LD B,$03`, and **one frame longer unless the flag
; is exactly 02:$56C5's `$11`** -- an ordinary explosion leaving small health,
; which is the commonest corpse in the game and the only one that is three.
!EN_EXP_BIG_N = $06
!EN_EXP_N     = $03
!EN_EXP_SHORT = $11
; 02:$56E9's `CP $FD`: an enemy whose *initial* health is this does not become a
; drop and does not free its slot. It clears the explosion and starts its AI
; again from counter 1, which is how the Metroid husks come back.
!EN_HP_RESPAWN = $FD
; The three drops, each as the `LD BC,d16` that sets both halves at once: B is
; the `dropType` the slot carries (+$0D) and C is the sprite it wears.
; 02:$5705, 02:$570A and 02:$5700.
!EN_DROP_SMALL = $01
!SPR_DROP_SMALL = $E0
!EN_DROP_LARGE = $02
!SPR_DROP_LARGE = $EC
!EN_DROP_MISSILE = $04
!SPR_DROP_MISSILE = $EE
; And the drop's own life, all of it from `enemy_animateDrop`: it is deleted on
; the counter reaching 02:$5697's `CP $B0`, blinks on 02:$569F's `AND $03` until
; 02:$569B's `CP $80` and on 02:$56A8's `AND $01` after it, and the blink is
; 02:$56AF's `XOR $01` on the sprite id -- so the two frames of a drop's
; animation are an even id and the odd one above it.
!EN_DROP_LIFE = $B0
!EN_DROP_FAST = $80
!EN_DROP_SLOW_M = $03
!EN_DROP_FAST_M = $01
!EN_DROP_BLINK = $01
; 02:$56F0's `AND $01`, the mask of the 50% drop-nothing roll. **The mask is the
; original's and the register it masks is not**: 02:$56ED reads `rDIV`, the Game
; Boy's free-running divider, and this machine has no counterpart. See
; `EnemyAnimateExplosion.becomeDrop` for what stands in and why.
!EN_DROP_ROLL = $01

; ---- Step 12f: the enemy AIs the slice dispatches ---------------------------
;
; Nine AIs, measured off the Game Boy rather than read out of the spawn lists:
; `zig build gbtrace -- ais 73400` hooks the `JP (HL)` at 02:$5650 that every AI
; goes through, over James's recording through Alpha 2's death. See
; `docs/slice.md`. Every number below is read out of the site that carries it
; and graded by `src/correspond.zig`.

; The enemies' tilemap probes, 02:$4608-$4DD2. Each probe presets
; `en_bgCollisionResult` ($C402) to one of four values -- the edge it tests, as
; a pair of set bits -- and clears the low one if no point it tests is solid.
; So a caller's `BIT n,A` on the result is "did that edge hit".
!EN_BG_DOWN   = $22             ; 02:$4A28 `LD A,$22`
!EN_BG_DOWN_CLR = $FD           ; 02:$4A25 `RES 1,(HL)`
!EN_BG_RIGHT  = $11             ; 02:$4783 `LD A,$11`
!EN_BG_RIGHT_CLR = $FE         ; 02:$47B1 `RES 0,(HL)`
!EN_BG_LEFT   = $44             ; 02:$495C `LD A,$44`
!EN_BG_LEFT_CLR = $FB          ; 02:$498A `RES 2,(HL)`
!EN_BG_UP     = $88             ; 02:$4D51 `LD A,$88`
!EN_BG_UP_CLR = $F7            ; 02:$4DAE `RES 3,(HL)`

; `crawler_turn` (02:$5895-$58DD): which way a crawler is going is the low
; nibble of `+$08 directionFlags` -- 0 right, 1 down, 2 left, 3 up -- and a turn
; also picks the sprite pair ($x0 horizontal, $x2 vertical) and overwrites the
; attribute byte with one flip: X for right and down, Y for left and up.
!CRAWL_SPR_VERT = $02           ; 02:$58B4 `ADD A,$02`
!CRAWL_ATTR_H   = $20           ; 02:$58A3 `LD A,$20`
!CRAWL_ATTR_V   = $40           ; 02:$58C8 `LD A,$40`

; `enAI_hopper` (02:$61DB): Hornoads, Autoads and Ramulkens.
; The rise is 02:$61E9's `CP $10` calls long, the fall is the same table read
; backwards from 02:$6256's `LD A,$0F`, and the sprite steps up on 02:$6218's
; fifth call. Every 02:$624C `CP $03` landings it turns round.
!HOP_ARC_N    = $10
!HOP_FALL_TOP = $0F
!HOP_FRAME_AT = $05
!HOP_TURN_N   = $03
; 02:$6289: a hopper that starts to the left of this faces the other way.
!HOP_FACE_X   = $C8
; 02:$6220 and 02:$6223: the Autoad's second frame requests its jump sound.
!SPR_AUTOAD_2 = $47
!SFX_AUTOAD_JUMP = $1A

; `enAI_rockIcicle` (02:$5542): six states, each a count and a sprite.
!ROCK_SPR_IDLE_1 = $34          ; 02:$5563 `LD A,$34`
!ROCK_SPR_IDLE_2 = $35          ; 02:$5574
!ROCK_SPR_MOVE_1 = $36          ; 02:$55A1, and 02:$5619's `CP $36`
!ROCK_IDLE_N  = $0B             ; 02:$556C `CP $0B`
!ROCK_WAIT_N  = $07             ; 02:$5584
!ROCK_EDGE_M  = $03             ; 02:$5595 `AND $03`
!ROCK_EDGE_N  = $04             ; 02:$559E
!ROCK_HANG_N  = $10             ; 02:$55C2
!ROCK_DROP_M  = $05             ; 02:$55D2 `AND $05`
!ROCK_FALL    = $04             ; 02:$55E2 `ADD A,$04`
!ROCK_FLOOR_Y = $A0             ; 02:$55F7 `CP $A0`
!SFX_HIT_GROUND = $11           ; 02:$55FA

; `enAI_gullugg` (02:$5CE0): three sprite frames, $D8 to 02:$5E02's `CP $DA`.
!GUL_SPR_1    = $D8             ; 02:$5E08 `LD (HL),$D8`
