; The projectile pass's own variables, absolute for the same reason the enemy
; pass's are: the direct page is full and none of these is read often.
!PrIndex      = $017E           ; $D032 `projectileIndex`
!PrSlot       = $017F           ; 2 - `hBeam_p` ($FFB7/$FFB8), as a byte offset
                                ;     rather than an address, because that is
                                ;     what X holds here
!PrType       = $0181           ; `hBeam_type` $FFB9
!PrWave       = $0182           ; `hBeam_waveIndex` $FFBA
!PrFrame      = $0183           ; `hBeam_frameCouter` $FFBB, the ROM's spelling
; `hTemp.a`, `.b` and `.c` ($FF98-$FF9A). **The two routines that use them do
; not agree on what they mean**, and that is the original's doing rather than a
; slip here: `samusShoot` puts the direction in `.b`, the Y offset in `.c` and
; the X offset in `.a`, while `handleProjectiles` puts the direction in `.a`, Y
; in `.b` and X in `.c`. So they are named for the HRAM byte, not for a meaning.
!PrTmpA       = $0184
!PrTmpB       = $0185
!PrTmpC       = $0186
!PrB          = $0187           ; the routines' B register
!PrC          = $0188           ; and their C
; The enemy sprite set's two pointers, and `drawEnemySprite`'s own scratch.
; `$C300` is what the cartridge parks a sprite id with no record at -- id $9A,
; the same dead slot the hitbox table has -- and the conversion turns it into
; `$FFFF`, which `DrawEnemySprite` refuses.
!EnMsPtrs     = $01BC           ; 3
!EnMsData     = $01BF           ; 3
!EnMsRec      = $01C2           ; 3 - the record being walked
!DrawEnY      = $01C5           ; `drawEnemy_yPos`
!DrawEnX      = $01C6           ; `drawEnemy_xPos`
!DrawEnSpr    = $01C7           ; `drawEnemy_sprite`
!DrawEnAttr   = $01C8           ; `drawEnemy_attr`, already masked to $F0
!MS_DEAD      = $FFFF
!PrTmpD       = $01B9           ; `ShotDirNibble`'s own, which the Game Boy does
                                ;   not need because its pad is already in this
                                ;   order
; `samus_onscreenYPos` ($D03B) and `samus_onscreenXPos` ($D03C).
;
; **These were `!SprY` and `!SprX` until Step 12b, and the aliasing was right
; until it was not.** `drawSamus_common` (01:$4DDF) writes each pair from one
; value -- `ldh [hSpriteXPixel],a / ld [samus_onscreenXPos],a` -- so while Samus
; was the only thing this engine drew, the sprite bytes *were* her on-screen
; position, and eight routines read them as such: `HandleCamera`'s four door
; triggers, three entries of the sprite collision, and the Senjoo's AI, which
; measures its distance from Samus.
;
; `drawProjectiles` writes `hSpriteXPixel` and does **not** write
; `samus_onscreenXPos`, so from the first frame a beam is in the air the two
; stop being the same number -- and those six readers would have been asking a
; projectile where Samus is. See `docs/bug_tracker.md`, 2026-09-09.
!OnscreenY    = $01BA           ; $D03B
!OnscreenX    = $01BB           ; $D03C
; What `HandleCamera`'s horizontal door triggers actually read, exported so the
; gate can assert it is Samus's byte and not the sprite's on a frame with a
; projectile in the air. A `Var*` on a *choice* rather than on a variable, which
; is what lets the fixture be watched failing against the aliased engine.
VarTriggerX   = !OnscreenX
VarOnscreenY  = !OnscreenY
!WeaponType   = $0189           ; $D08D, the type of the projectile being handled
!WeaponDir    = $018A           ; $D012, and its direction
!BeamCool     = $018B           ; $D00D `samusBeamCooldown`
; `hSpriteAttr` ($FFC7). The other three of the original's sprite quartet are
; already here as `!SpriteId`, `!SprX` and `!SprY`; this one arrives with the
; first caller that draws an object no metasprite record describes.
!SprAttr      = $018C
; How far `LoadEnemyBox` widens the enemy's horizontal edges. **$05 for Samus
; and $00 for a projectile**, which is the one place the two hitbox tests differ
; in the box they resolve: 00:$3388's `SUB $05`/`ADD $05` are Samus's own
; half-width and 00:$3250, the projectile's copy, has no counterpart.
!ColPad       = $018D
; `collision_weaponDir` ($D060) and `enSprCollision.weaponDir` ($C469).
; `EnemyCollisionResults`' note has said since Step 10 that the port has no
; variable for it because only a projectile writes one; this is B5 adding it, on
; both sides of the one-frame handshake.
!CollWeaponDir = $018E
!SprWeaponDir  = $018F
; One object on its way into the OAM shadow, in Game Boy screen coordinates:
; `PutObject`'s arguments, and exactly the four bytes a Game Boy OAM entry is.
!ObjY         = $0190
!ObjX         = $0191
!ObjTile      = $0192
!ObjAttr      = $0193
; ($0194 was `!CannonGfx`, which cannon the toggle last asked for, until 1.0
; Step 8a put the toggle through `LoadGraphics`.)
; The first weapon type `EnemyDamageOrDrop` was handed that it has no arm for.
; $FF until one happens, the way `!EnUnhandledState` is - a number the gate can
; print instead of a silent wrong turn.
!PrUnhandled  = $0195
; The eleven tables Step 12b converts, resolved once at boot. Three bytes each,
; and a table-driven resolve rather than eleven copies of the same nine
; instructions - the same trade `SpriteBlobs` made, with sixteen-bit variable
; addresses because these are not in the direct page.
!ShotDirsA    = $0196           ; 3
!ShotPriA     = $0199           ; 3
!CanXA        = $019C           ; 3
!CanYPoseA    = $019F           ; 3
!CanYAimA     = $01A2           ; 3
!WaveSpdA     = $01A5           ; 3
!MissSpdA     = $01A8           ; 3
!MissTileA    = $01AB           ; 3
!MissAttrA    = $01AE           ; 3
!BeamSndA     = $01B1           ; 3
!WpnDmgA      = $01B4           ; 3
; The missile speed table's length, because the reader wants the entry two from
; the end and the original writes that as an absolute `LD A,($51C1)` at
; 01:$51D6. Kept the way `!BombArcLen` is rather than baked, so the number comes
; off the blob and not out of this file.
!MissSpdLen   = $01B7           ; 2

